{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Regime-dependent bias: when spot is trending up strongly (multi-day rally / breakout), the model systematically under-forecasts the medium-to-long horizon (≈day 15–30), missing the continuation and step-up (true ends ~+5 to +10 above forecast in the Nov 18–21 samples). Apply an upward drift add-on in strong uptrend regimes that increases with horizon.",
    "Post-spike overshoot: immediately after a sharp jump, the model often projects too much mean reversion / sell-off (forecasts fall into low 60s) while realized prices stay elevated and then grind higher. Damp negative corrections after upside shocks; cap forecast drawdowns for days 10–30 following a spike.",
    "Excess volatility in the tail: the forecast path is too wavy versus realized (oscillates high then collapses), especially beyond ~2 weeks. Apply smoothing/variance shrinkage to day 15–30 forecasts (pull toward a trend-following baseline rather than large swings).",
    "Horizon shape error: short horizon (≈day 1–10) is relatively well calibrated (small errors), but error magnitude grows with horizon and is mostly negative in rising regimes. Use horizon-weighted bias correction: near-zero adjustment for days 1–7, increasing positive adjustment for days 15–30 when trend is positive.",
    "Level-dependent underpricing at high levels: when prices are in the upper 60s/low 70s, the model tends to anchor too low and fails to reach the new plateau. Add a level-based uplift when the last observed price is above a threshold (e.g., >68) and momentum is positive."
  ]
}
